Independent blind design comparison. Read-only; no implementation, delegation, prior ratings or other grader output. Compare baseline/A/B/C for each of three scenes. Return twelve scene/variant rows, each aggregating provided text sizes/times. Use six dimensions, null where behavior cannot be judged. Choose strongest coherent approach, consent and opening variants. Baseline may win; there is no requested score.
FROZEN RUBRIC:
## Rubric: score the screen AND its components

Grade each dimension 0–10. Aesthetic score A = mean of hierarchy, typography/spacing, surface/color coherence. Functional score F = mean of task clarity, feedback/recovery, accessibility. Overall = 0.4A + 0.6F, rounded to one decimal. Apply the same six dimensions to each screen and each meaningful component (orb, tabs, option, input, card, button, modal). Mark genuinely inapplicable dimensions N/A and normalize; never infer an interaction score from a screenshot.

- **6 — usable but visibly rough:** task succeeds; inconsistent spacing, competing emphasis, or weak feedback is obvious. No data loss or unreachable primary action.
- **8 — clean and dependable:** one clear next action; consistent type/spacing/surface roles; responsive press/loading/error states; 44pt targets; text enlargement, contrast and reduced motion checked. Minor polish remains.
- **9 — premium and effortless:** all 8 criteria plus continuity through transitions, contextual recovery, concise humane copy, restrained delight, no distracting layout shifts; real phone and assistive-technology checks passed.
- **10 — exemplary for this task:** all 9 criteria; repeated novice use and representative accessibility/performance checks find no material friction. Reserve for demonstrated outcomes, not taste or resemblance to another app.
- **0–5:** 0 absent/unusable; 2 major path broken; 4 task possible only with workarounds; 5 works but a serious clarity/accessibility issue remains.

Hard gates: blocked or materially unanswered task/data loss/error disguised as success => overall at most 4. Essential unreadable content, inaccessible primary control or motion preference ignored => at most 5. Unverified runtime/native behavior => provisional grade, cannot certify 9/10. Averages never erase a failed gate.

Loop acceptance: every core screen and critical component >=8, A and F each >=8, no hard-gate failure. Record before/after screenshot at 390×844, action and result, normal/200% text, reduced motion, empty/loading/error/selected state and any untested boundary. Change one issue at a time; keep every failed observation. Browser checks do not prove native haptics, voice, keyboard or screen reader behavior.



Authorized loop threshold is A >=9 and F >=9 for every screen and meaningful component from both independent graders. This supersedes the report's >=8 acceptance sentence; its evidence requirements and hard gates remain unchanged. Native and physical assistive-technology coverage is unverified, so no 9/10 certification is permitted on that evidence. Grade honestly; the target is not a requested score.


Stage clarification from firstmate inbox004: continue scoring the browser implementation provisionally until every screen and component reaches A>=9 and F>=9 there; firstmate will then arrange the physical iPhone/VoiceOver pass. For this stage, judge the demonstrated browser behavior across the full numeric range and record missing physical/native proof separately, rather than lowering every browser score solely for that deferred boundary. This does NOT certify a full/native9/10 or waive a browser accessibility defect. Keep all other anchors, dimensions and failure gates unchanged. Never award a target score merely to complete the loop.

Current baseline browser evidence: 127 tests,270 layout/text states,1686 targets,489 task contrast checks,16 entrances,actual local-model reading flow,normal and golden opening about60fps/max16.8ms,skip/close/earlyShare return and reduced-motion checks pass. Physical/native checks deferred and separately unverified. These are baseline facts, not proof of prototype behavior.
All proposals are throwaway HTML with selected Fraunces/Manrope and existing Open mist. No prototype implements model submission, persisted consent, speech, storage, native keyboard or accessibility semantics equivalent to production. No credit for assumed implementation. Captures verified loaded fonts,no page errors or horizontal overflow. 500px views model constrained space, not a physical keyboard. Dark space below500px in sheets is letterboxing outside product. Attached captures plus public/concepts/expansion-34.html and docs/ux-loop/expansion-34/checks.json may be read. Do not read other review files or source.
APPROACH (390x844 normal200): baseline current product 'Come closer' automatically advances after650ms to optional speech/type input. Screenshot alone hides that timer; it does not await an unknown gesture. A removes the interstitial and opens typed composer immediately, with optional Speak instead. B holds a deliberate mode choice, clear Type a question and quiet Speak a question. C names automatic transition to voice with a progress line and immediately available Type instead;650ms then voice. Capture mode freezes C, interactive prototype runs it. Judge friction/clarity and visual hierarchy, not microphone behavior.
CONSENT (390x500 normal200, same complete pottery question, submit scrolled into view): baseline explicit named provider consent lies above question; unchecked means disabled Ask. A retains that structure and adds local reason plus Review consent link beside disabled Ask. B replaces disabled submit with actionable Review AI consent; its included review state shows full named disclosure/checkbox then Return to question after opt-in. C puts named consent before entering composer, Continue to question disabled until explicit checkbox and Not now available. None authorizes sending before real explicit consent. Stored draft/guard/focus behavior is not proven by prototype. Compare accessible next-action clarity against added steps and text.
OPENING (390x844): baseline actual current staged900ms charge/200ms split/700ms forecast settle. A fills a vertical split with bounded warm light and parts glass halves. B lifts a glass cap above horizontal light while lower sphere/stand stay grounded. C uses a fine luminous fracture then mist release without moving glass fragments. All keep one restrained release, skip and reduced-motion intent; prototypes only illustrate normal timing, not golden or reduced behavior. Baseline screenshots come from an actual run at approximate0/500/900ms; alternatives are paused CSS animations at exact0/500/900/1000ms, so frames are observational sequences, not synchronized pixel comparisons. Opening source can be inspected for timing, but static captures cannot certify smoothness/FPS. Judge material continuity, silhouette, implied weight and restrained reward beat, honestly mark motion limits. Game references inform anticipation/release/payoff, not copied art. Do not treat an unimplemented proposal as verified production.
Return schema JSON, concise concrete findings and tradeoffs.

